  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on self-reported behavioral intentions and survey responses rather than observed, real-world transit choices.",
      "what": "This study extends the theory of planned behavior to analyze how prior riding experience affects passenger acceptance of autonomous buses, incorporating three dimensions of perceived risk. Using survey data, the model compares behavioral intentions, risk perceptions, and sociodemographic variations between individuals who have ridden an autonomous bus and those who have not.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that first-hand experience shifts the drivers of acceptance: inexperienced riders are more influenced by social norms and operational or environmental risks, whereas experienced riders are driven more by their own attitudes and perceived behavioral control. This provides a empirical basis for tailoring public transit promotion strategies depending on whether the target population has actual experience with autonomous transit."
    }
